A||||4||88formula||How many are using the 3.4 oil pan in their 88 fiero’s.||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||06-21-2001||06:36 PM||||I would like to do this because I don't like the small diameter of the 88 2.8 oil pump pickup tube. I like the much larger diameter of the 3.4 oil pump pickup tube. If I use the 3.4 oil pump than I have to use the 3.4 pan or modify the 2.8 88 pan. I don't want to start cutting the baffles in my 88 2.8 pan.<P>I have a high volume 1990 3.1 oil pump by the way.<P>Does the 3.4 pan clear the exhaust system? Does it stick below the engine cradle? I know it doesn’t clear the 88 front engine mount bracket but that I can deal with.<P>A high volume 2.8 oil pump might give you great oil pressure but it will still move less oil volume than a high volume 3.4 pump because the oil pickup tube is smaller. Oil pressure and oil volume are not the same thing. You could have great pressure but not so great volume and it could be the other way around.  I want both.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||06-21-2001||06:44 PM||||I had to use the 3.4L ribbed aluminum pan that came with my FWD 3.4.  The Fiero pan will not fit a FWD 3.4L  It is about 1" deeper, but clears everything fine.  You will have to notch the engine's front "L" shaped steel mount to have clearance for the oil pan's front corner.  What to do will be evident when you get there.<P>Of course, I hear Camaro blocks will accept the Fiero oil pan without problems.  Can anyone elaborate on this?? ||||reg||10
Z||000002||88formula||06-21-2001||06:56 PM||||I can bolt up either the fiero's pan or the 95 Camaro 3.4 pan, they both work with my timing cover. I guess if you can use a huge aluminum pan like that in your fiero than I can use the Camaro 3.4 pan in my fiero. I’ve seen your engine fierosound but I never thought about it before. The Motor mount bracket is no problem.<P>Thanks.    <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||06-21-2001||09:27 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pump_pickup_1.JPG"> <BR>See the size difference between the pickups. The sizes are .565" for the 2.8 pickup and .640 for the 3.4 pickup at their smallest points in the bends.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||00lE||06-22-2001||04:09 AM||||I used the FWD aluminum pan on my swap in an 88.  I had to grind down the AC boss on the pan( they were not used anyway) and a little bit on the fins under the front of the pan to clear the mount bracket lip.  It really was not hard at all since the aluminum was quite soft, perhaps the Fiero front engine mount brackets differ somewhat between years?<P>This sorry attempt at a picture edit may show what was needed in my case.  The arrow points to the AC boss and the red mark indicates the area where the mount lip needs to clear.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/oil_pan_modification.jpg"> <BR> <p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
